<section class="section section-pad" id="use-cases" style="background:transparent;">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-kicker">Use cases</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Three scenarios where federation is the only option.</h2>
    <p class="section-sub" style="max-width:70ch;">
      No identity provider mediates these flows. Cross-domain validation depends entirely on PKI
      federation, and each scenario exercises different components of the governance function Φ.
    </p>

    <div class="usecase-grid">
      <article class="challenge-card usecase-card">
        <div class="usecase-tag">NPE / real-time</div>
        <h3>Machine-to-machine mTLS</h3>
        <p>
          A service in Domain A authenticates a client certificate issued in Domain C. The relying
          party builds and validates the chain against its own anchors, at connection time.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase-exercises">
          Exercises: <span class="phi">scope</span> (clientAuth), <span class="phi">time</span>
          (t_now), <span class="phi">crypto_floor</span>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="challenge-card usecase-card">
        <div class="usecase-tag">PAdES / long-term</div>
        <h3>Document signature validation</h3>
        <p>
          A PDF signed in Domain C is opened in Domain A. The signer cannot anticipate every
          verifying domain, so the verifier must discover the cross-certificate on its own.
          Long-term validation: signing-time semantics and historical revocation.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase-exercises">
          Exercises: <span class="phi">scope</span> (document signing), <span class="phi">time</span
          >, <span class="phi">revoked</span>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="challenge-card usecase-card">
        <div class="usecase-tag">S/MIME / role inversion</div>
        <h3>Coalition messaging</h3>
        <p>
          Signed and encrypted mail across domains. Encryption inverts the roles: the sender must
          validate the foreign recipient's certificate before transmitting.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase-exercises">
          Exercises: <span class="phi">ku</span> (keyEncipherment), <span class="phi">name</span>,
          <span class="phi">pol⁺/pol⁻</span>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

